WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:03.060
This series takes a closer look at each part

00:03.080 --> 00:06.000
of HASTE. In this episode: understanding

00:06.020 --> 00:08.580
the outputs, from how far to trust the damage

00:08.600 --> 00:11.270
map, to what you can download and share.

00:15.300 --> 00:18.810
A damage map is only useful if you know how far to trust it.

00:19.273 --> 00:22.523
So HASTE builds in an independent check, Building Validation.

00:23.610 --> 00:25.580
It draws a random sample of buildings,

00:25.670 --> 00:28.120
200 here, and hides the model's predictions.

00:29.920 --> 00:32.650
The reviewer judges each building on the imagery alone,

00:32.780 --> 00:34.590
flipping between before and after.

00:35.323 --> 00:37.013
This first one is a small shed,

00:37.103 --> 00:38.913
too hard to make out so it's Unknown.

00:39.730 --> 00:42.900
The next two are clearer, one standing and one burned.

00:44.813 --> 00:46.003
Number keys make it quick.

00:47.340 --> 00:49.750
We'll skip ahead through the rest and save.

00:57.633 --> 01:01.003
The validation report compares those labels with the model.

01:03.337 --> 01:05.847
97% of the scored buildings are right.

01:07.153 --> 01:10.663
41 damaged buildings and 150 intact ones match.

01:12.497 --> 01:14.577
Three damaged buildings were missed and

01:14.597 --> 01:17.107
three intact ones were flagged as Damaged.

01:17.847 --> 01:20.507
None of our 16 edits fell in this random sample,

01:20.527 --> 01:22.657
so it's still an independent test.

01:23.367 --> 01:25.187
The assessment report answers the

01:25.207 --> 01:27.017
question responders ask first.

01:27.367 --> 01:28.717
How many buildings are damaged?

01:29.547 --> 01:31.977
The saved map counts 482.

01:33.497 --> 01:35.787
Separately, from the random sample alone,

01:36.077 --> 01:37.777
HASTE estimates that 433

01:37.797 --> 01:40.697
of the 1,937 buildings larger

01:40.717 --> 01:42.847
than 50 square meters are damaged,

01:43.190 --> 01:47.840
with a 95 percent confidence interval of 326 to 540.

01:48.510 --> 01:51.040
The map's count falls inside that range,

01:51.070 --> 01:52.360
so the two are consistent.

01:54.567 --> 01:58.237
To use the map outside HASTE, download it as a GeoPackage,

01:58.547 --> 02:00.857
the raw predictions, or a saved version.

02:05.460 --> 02:07.320
Each row is one building footprint,

02:07.340 --> 02:09.010
with its area and its prediction.

02:09.700 --> 02:13.170
Saved versions add the model's class, any analyst override,

02:13.260 --> 02:14.800
and the final edited class,

02:14.820 --> 02:17.530
so every fix from episode three is traceable.

02:17.860 --> 02:21.210
It opens in QGIS, ArcGIS, or Python.

02:22.667 --> 02:25.167
Finally, publishing packages the model's

02:25.187 --> 02:27.777
damage GeoPackage, the building footprints,

02:28.047 --> 02:30.057
a mask of the valid image area,

02:30.127 --> 02:32.897
and the processed imagery into a shared dataset.

02:34.970 --> 02:37.700
HASTE drafts the description from the assessment report.

02:42.210 --> 02:44.060
Published datasets get their own catalog,

02:44.230 --> 02:47.160
where your team can find them and download each file.

02:51.967 --> 02:55.337
HASTE is open source under the MIT license.

02:55.807 --> 02:58.917
Deploy it to Azure with a single azd up command,

02:59.007 --> 03:01.217
or run it locally with Docker Compose.

03:01.667 --> 03:04.597
Find it on GitHub at microsoft/haste.
